.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt VCONSOLE.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vconsole.conf
.Nd console keyboard settings applied at boot
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /etc/vconsole.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr init 8
reads this optional file once, before starting the first console session.
Each line is an assignment of the form
.Li NAME=value .
Blank lines and lines whose first non-space character is a hash or
semicolon are ignored.
Spaces around the name and value are removed.
A whole value may be enclosed in single or double quotes.
There is no shell execution, expansion, escaping, or continuation.
The last assignment of a name wins.
Unknown names are ignored.
.Pp
KEYMAP and KEYMAP_TOGGLE use systemd's variable names.
KEYMAP_OPTIONS, REPEAT_DELAY, REPEAT_RATE and NUMLOCK are Rach2 extensions.
.Bl -tag -width KEYMAP_OPTIONS
.It Va KEYMAP
The base text keymap, sought under
.Pa /usr/share/kbd/keymaps .
An empty value selects
.Li us .
.It Va KEYMAP_TOGGLE
An optional additional map loaded after the base map, for a layout switch.
For example,
.Li alt-shift .
.It Va KEYMAP_OPTIONS
Up to sixteen space-separated overlay names loaded after KEYMAP_TOGGLE.
For example,
.Li caps-ctrl .
.It Va REPEAT_DELAY
An integer delay in milliseconds, from 1 through 60000.
.It Va REPEAT_RATE
An integer repeat rate per second, from 1 through 1000.
When both repeat settings are empty, the repeat configuration is unchanged.
When only one is set,
.Xr kbdrate 8
uses its default for the other.
.It Va NUMLOCK
.Li yes
or
.Li 1
sets Num Lock and its default;
.Li no
or
.Li 0
clears them.
An empty value leaves the setting unchanged.
.El
.Pp
Map names start with an ASCII letter or digit and otherwise contain only
letters, digits, periods, underscores, plus signs and hyphens.
Paths and tool options are refused.
Values are at most 255 bytes and lines at most 1023 bytes.
.Pp
Init runs
.Li loadkeys -u
with the base map, the toggle map and then the overlays;
then it runs
.Li kbdrate
and
.Li setleds
for the settings present.
Each command has standard input on
.Pa /dev/console .
A malformed or unreadable file causes init to load
.Li us
instead of applying any partial settings.
After a command fails, init stops the sequence and tries
.Li loadkeys -u us ,
since a failed load may already have changed part of the keyboard map.
Login still starts after a failure.
A missing file leaves the kernel's defaults unchanged.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
KEYMAP=kg
KEYMAP_TOGGLE=alt-shift
KEYMAP_OPTIONS="caps-ctrl"
REPEAT_DELAY=250
REPEAT_RATE=30
NUMLOCK=yes
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr loadkeys 1 ,
.Xr setleds 1 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr kbdrate 8
